An adversarial agent re-examined today's failures and my entries. Two of my
conclusions were wrong. Both corrections verified here independently rather than
accepted on report.

exp6901 was not killed by the wall-clock cap. Its artifact is terminal and honest --
status blocked, verdict complete_blocked_..., correctly refusing the flagged exp6900
input -- but _artifact_is_finished tests status in _BOOTSTRAP_STATUSES first, and
that frozenset contains "blocked" (research_conductor.py:2717, verified). So the
conductor treated a finished artifact as an unfinished bootstrap and re-ran it, three
times, each rerun reproducing the same blocked artifact. The 4803s cap kill was how
rerun three ended, not the cause. It failed three times, not twice as I reported.

The cascade was also correct, contrary to my framing. exp6902 through 6904 gate on a
readiness score and an event count that a run refusing its input cannot produce, so
they block with or without the retirement.

Separately, _classify_retirement exists to distinguish merit from environmental
failure and is dead code -- confirmed by grep, no callers outside its definition and
a verification script. That answers the merit-versus-budget question precisely: the
machinery makes no such distinction because the code that would is never called.

The most important correction is to my own recommendation. The agent ran the A/B my
entry said the fix required, over 7,078 artifacts, and gating DURATION_TOO_SHORT on a
declaration-field test rescues genuine fabrication candidates. Verified directly here:
experiment_1003_spilled_energy_live_gpu_v4 claims a live GPU run in 0.066s and
experiment_1035_dualgpu_rocm_v3 in 0.048s, neither with a marker in any declaration
field, so both would be rescued from the floor by the discriminator I kept
recommending. Adding title and verdict checks still leaves about 174 rescued. No
field-based discriminator separates my false positives from real fabrications. The
mechanism and the 46% direction stand; the remedy does not, and filed-not-built is now
the verified-correct status rather than caution.

Also corrected: exp6863 at 38.4s escaped the floor via
_is_precondition_check_only_blocked, not by its duration, so it was never evidence
about the threshold; and the forced-sequence span I quoted is effectively n=1.

Nothing was shipped. The _artifact_is_finished fix is identified with its exact
tension -- blocked is in that set so transient blocks retry, so any fix must key on
the verdict -- and left for an owner with a mutation test rather than applied to a
live conductor from a shared checkout.

